 ],
 "literal": "In my small house, clutching the fine pillar, your son / where he is, even if you ask, I do not know, indeed, / like a stone cave that a tiger stayed in and left, / the womb that gave birth to him is this indeed, / he will appear surely on the battlefield.",
 "translation": "Clutching the elegant pillar of my small house, you ask, 'Where is your son?' I do not know where he is. But look at this womb that gave birth to him—it is like a stone cave once occupied and then abandoned by a tiger. He will surely be found on the battlefield!",
 "modern": "என் சிறிய வீட்டின் அழகிய தூணைப் பற்றிக்கொண்டு, 'உன் மகன் எங்கே?' என்று கேட்கிறாய். அவன் எங்கு இருக்கிறான் என்று எனக்குத் தெரியாது. ஆனால், அவனைப் பெற்றெடுத்த என் வயிறு இதோ இருக்கிறது; இது புலி தங்கிவிட்டுச் சென்ற கற்குகையைப் போன்றது. அவன் போர்க்களத்தில்தான் இருப்பான்; அங்கே அவனைக் காணலாம்.",
 "explanation": "A mother is speaking to a visitor who has come to her small home inquiring about the whereabouts of her young son. She replies that she does not know where he is at this moment, but proudly points to her own womb, comparing it to a rocky mountain cave from which a tiger has departed. This powerful metaphor implies that her son is a fierce tiger who has grown up and gone out to perform his heroic duty. The poem belongs to the vākai tiṇai (victory and heroism), specifically the theme of ēṟāṇ mullai, which celebrates the courage and martial pride of a warrior's mother. Key words that carry this heroic sentiment include 'புலி' (tiger), 'கல்அளை' (stone cave), and 'போர்க்களத்து' (battlefield).",
